First, clarify the score function. For each item j (column j of D), we compute a score
f(D_{*,j}) = sum_{i=1}^n x_{π(i), j}
where π is a fixed permutation of the n users. Since π is just a reordering, this is simply the sum of the n entries in column j, i.e., f(D_{*,j}) = ∑_{i=1}^n D_{i,j}.

Neighboring datasets D and D' differ in at most one row. Therefore, for any fixed column j, the score difference between D and D' is
|f(D_{*,j}) − f(D'_{*,j})| = |∑_{i=1}^n D_{i,j} − ∑_{i=1}^n D'_{i,j}| = |D_{r,j} − D'_{r,j}|
for the single differing row r. Since entries lie in [0,1], this per-column sensitivity is at most 1:
Δ = max_{neighbors D,D'} |f(D_{*,j}) − f(D'_{*,j})| ≤ 1.

We use the “report noisy max” mechanism: independently sample Z_j ~ Laplace(2/ε) for each j, and output argmax_j (f(D_{*,j}) + Z_j). The classical result is that if each score function has ℓ1-sensitivity Δ, then adding i.i.d. Laplace noise with scale b = 2Δ/ε to each score and returning the argmax is ε-differentially private. Here Δ = 1, so scale b = 2/ε matches exactly the condition.

Therefore, the mechanism satisfies ε-differential privacy.

\boxed{yes}
